  title="How to Triage Maintenance Work Orders So the Right Ones Move First"
  description="A four tier system for sorting maintenance requests by consequence, the intake fields that make it work, and the batching rules that cut trips and cost."
>
<p>Work order triage is the practice of sorting incoming maintenance requests by consequence rather than by arrival time, so the ones that damage the property or the tenancy get handled first and everything else gets batched. The working rule is four tiers: life safety and habitability, active damage, functional problems, and cosmetic or deferred. A first in first out queue treats a dripping faucet and a leaking supply line as equals, and that is how a $200 repair becomes a $4,000 one. This article gives you the tier definitions, the intake fields that make triage possible, and the batching rules that keep the routine work from consuming your week.</p>

<h2>The four tiers</h2>

<h3>Tier 1: Life safety and habitability</h3>

<p>These are the ones where waiting is not an option, and several of them are not maintenance calls at all.</p>

<ul>
<li>Smell of gas, or a carbon monoxide alarm sounding. This is a 911 and gas utility call, immediately, before anyone calls a contractor. Tenant leaves the building first.</li>
<li>Any active electrical hazard: sparking, burning smell, a hot outlet or panel, exposed conductors. Power to the circuit off, electrician engaged.</li>
<li>No heat in freezing weather, or no cooling during extreme heat, especially with elderly tenants, infants, or medical needs in the unit.</li>
<li>No running water, or no hot water at all.</li>
<li>Sewage backing up into the unit.</li>
<li>An unsecured unit: a door that will not lock, a broken exterior door, a ground floor window that will not close after a break in.</li>
<li>Anything structural that has visibly moved or is holding water, a sagging ceiling in particular.</li>
</ul>

<p>These get routed the same day they come in. To be plain about our own role, Upkeep is not an after hours emergency service and does not sell 24/7 dispatch. We confirm requests within 2 business hours and schedule routine work within 48 hours. A true middle of the night Tier 1 needs the utility, the fire department, or an on call specialist, and every portfolio should have those numbers written on the tenant handout before they are needed rather than found during the event.</p>

<h3>Tier 2: Active damage</h3>

<p>Nothing is unsafe, but the property is getting worse every hour it waits. These are the highest return calls in maintenance because the cost curve is steep.</p>

<ul>
<li>Any active water: a supply line weeping, a toilet leaking at the base, a water heater seeping, a roof leak during rain, a tub or shower leaking into the ceiling below</li>
<li>An overflowing or slow draining main line</li>
<li>A refrigerator or freezer failure, which becomes a food loss claim and a smell problem quickly</li>
<li>An exterior envelope opening, missing shingles after a storm, a broken window pane, a failed door seal in weather</li>
<li>Pest activity that spreads if untouched</li>
</ul>

<p>Water is the whole category, really. A slow leak behind a wall for two weeks is drywall, insulation, framing, flooring, and remediation. The same leak caught on day one is a fitting. Any request that mentions water, damp, drip, stain, or smell gets read as Tier 2 until somebody proves otherwise.</p>

<h3>Tier 3: Functional</h3>

<p>The tenant's daily life is degraded, nothing is deteriorating. A dishwasher that will not drain, one burner out on a range, a garbage disposal jam, an HVAC unit that cools but not well, a closet door off its track, a leaky faucet that drips into the drain rather than into the cabinet, a bad GFCI.</p>

<p>These belong on the schedule, not on the phone. Target a few business days, and batch them, which is where the efficiency comes from.</p>

<h3>Tier 4: Cosmetic and deferred</h3>

<p>Scuffed paint, a sticking window, a loose cabinet knob, a fence board, a screen. Real, worth doing, and worth doing all at once. Hold them and knock the list out in a single visit, or roll them into the next turn if the lease is ending soon.</p>

<p>Deferring is not ignoring. A Tier 4 that sits for a year with no communication becomes a tenant retention problem, and a tenant who does not believe requests get handled stops reporting the Tier 2 items too. That is the actual risk of a slow queue, and it is expensive.</p>

<h2>The intake that makes triage possible</h2>

<p>You cannot triage what you cannot read. "Sink broken" tells you nothing about which tier it belongs in, and figuring it out costs a phone call, a voicemail, and a day. Require these fields on every request:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Location</strong>, which room and which fixture</li>
<li><strong>What is happening</strong>, in the tenant's own words</li>
<li><strong>Is water actively running or standing right now</strong>, yes or no. One question, and it sorts Tier 2 out of the pile instantly.</li>
<li><strong>When it started</strong></li>
<li><strong>Photos or a short video.</strong> Not optional. A photo resolves the tier question, tells the tech what parts to bring, and prevents the wasted trip that comes from guessing.</li>
<li><strong>Access instructions</strong>, pets, gate codes, work schedule, whether the tenant needs to be present</li>
</ul>

<p>A structured intake form does this automatically and a phone message does not, which is the main practical argument for pushing tenants to a form. Ours is at the <a href="/work-order/">service request page</a>, and what tenants can expect after they submit is on the <a href="/tenants/">tenants page</a>.</p>

<h2>The rules that keep the queue moving</h2>

<h3>Re-tier on arrival, always</h3>

<p>The tenant's urgency and the property's urgency are different things, in both directions. A tenant may report a ceiling stain casually. A tenant may report a broken dishwasher as an emergency. Neither is a criticism of the tenant. Triage is your job, not theirs.</p>

<h3>Set a standing approval limit</h3>

<p>Give your vendor a dollar figure below which they proceed and document, without calling for authorization. Most portfolios find that the approval delay costs more than the items ever did, because a tech standing in a unit waiting on a text is the most expensive labor you buy.</p>

<h3>Batch Tier 3 and 4 by geography and by property</h3>

<p>Two small items at the same address should never be two visits. Neither should two items three blocks apart on the same afternoon. Batching is where a maintenance budget actually gets cut, and it costs nothing to implement beyond a little patience on the non urgent stuff.</p>

<h3>Watch for the repeat</h3>

<p>The same unit, the same system, three times in a year, is not three work orders. It is one replacement decision being avoided one repair at a time. Track by unit and by system, and when the pattern shows up, price the replacement against the running repair total. Our guide on <a href="/articles/how-long-do-home-systems-last/">how long home systems last</a> is a useful reality check on whether you are repairing something that is simply at the end of its life.</p>

<h3>Close the loop with the tenant, even on the ones you are deferring</h3>

<p>"We have this, it is scheduled for Thursday" costs one message and prevents a second call, an escalation, and a review. Silence is what makes people call again.</p>

<h3>Document every job the same way</h3>

<p>Before photos, after photos, itemized invoice. It settles disputes about condition later, it supports the deposit conversation at move out, and it is what an owner needs to trust that the work happened. The line between damage and ordinary wear is where most deposit disputes live, and photos are how you win that conversation. We covered where that line sits under Tennessee practice in <a href="/articles/wear-and-tear-vs-damage-tennessee/">wear and tear versus damage</a>.</p>

<h2>What triage buys you</h2>

<p>Three things, and they compound. Fewer small problems become big ones, because Tier 2 stops sitting behind Tier 4. Fewer trips, because everything that can wait travels together. And fewer angry calls, because the tenant knows where their request stands even when the answer is next week.</p>

<p>If your coordinators are spending their days as a switchboard between six vendors, the problem is usually not the volume of requests. It is that everything arrives at the same priority and gets dispatched one call at a time. How we take intake, triage, and documentation off a management team's plate is on the <a href="/property-managers/">property managers page</a>, and our standard workflow is on <a href="/our-process/">our process</a>.</p>
